Fillets: a rounding ending on a periodic face along the line of whose seam
an arc at the corner's vertex runs (mitcad#235).

The case: a bar across a bore, its top edge rounded, ending on the bore's
wall; the bore's seam runs up from the corner's vertex along the line of
the bar's side edge on the wall (the edge continues the seam). The cut of
the rounding's end on the wall runs from its contact on the bar's top to
its contact on the bar's side, on that line; the wall it uncovers lies on
both sides of the line from that contact up to the vertex, so the seam
goes on down to the contact. PerformOneCorner took the cut in the period
of the side edge's curve on the wall (0211's half period from its first
point), a period away from the wall's domain, and nothing stood for the
seam below the vertex: the wall's face was unorientable (its wire not
closed in 2d), and the solid's volume came out wrong. Where the side edge
was split off the seam keeping both curves on the wall (as a boolean join leaves
it), the pseudo-seam code (OCC32) made the piece a seam with its curves
the wrong way round.

- ChFi3d_ArcAlongSeamLine: an arc of the face at the end, used once by it,
  from the corner's vertex along an isoline of the face's surface that is
  the line of a seam of the face with that vertex.
- PerformOneCorner, a cut stored whole (not split at a seam crossing),
  both of its points on arcs, not OnSame and not ending on or along the
  face at the end: where one point's arc runs along the seam's line, the
  cut's curve on the face is put in the period of the other point's arc;
  where its end on the line then lies a period from the arc's curve, the
  arc's part from the point to the vertex is stored as a curve on the face
  twice, with the arc's curve the way the face uses the arc and translated
  by the period the other way, between the vertex and the stripe's point:
  a piece of the seam. The OCC32 pseudo-seam code leaves that arc alone.

Before, the result was invalid. Test: a bar's top edge rounded where the
bar meets a tube's bore at its seam, as fused and copied (geometry
test_fillet_results).

